1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,218
<font color="#FFFFFF">"This is NeuPL, or Neural Population Learning,</font>

2
00:00:05,298 --> 00:00:10,016
<font color="#FFFFFF">published at ICLR 2022 by researchers at University
College London and DeepMind.</font>

3
00:00:10,096 --> 00:00:14,813
<font color="#FFFFFF">Learning to play strategy games like StarCraft or
poker requires discovering a whole population of diverse policies,</font>

4
00:00:14,893 --> 00:00:19,611
<font color="#FFFFFF">not just one.</font>

5
00:00:19,691 --> 00:00:24,409
<font color="#FFFFFF">The usual recipe grows this population by
iteratively training a fresh policy to beat the previous ones,</font>

6
00:00:24,489 --> 00:00:29,207
<font color="#FFFFFF">but in real games that approach wastes compute
relearning basic skills at every step and</font>

7
00:00:29,287 --> 00:00:34,004
<font color="#FFFFFF">produces under-trained policies when budgets run out.</font>

8
00:00:34,084 --> 00:00:38,802
<font color="#FFFFFF">NeuPL solves both problems by representing the
entire population inside a single conditional neural network,</font>

9
00:00:38,882 --> 00:00:43,600
<font color="#FFFFFF">so skills transfer freely across policies while
still enjoying convergence guarantees."</font>

10
00:00:43,980 --> 00:00:47,943
<font color="#FFFFFF">"Classical population-learning methods such as
Policy Space Response Oracles grow a set</font>

11
00:00:48,023 --> 00:00:51,985
<font color="#FFFFFF">of strategies by repeatedly training a new policy
to best-respond to a mixture over the</font>

12
00:00:52,065 --> 00:00:56,028
<font color="#FFFFFF">existing ones.</font>

13
00:00:56,108 --> 00:01:00,071
<font color="#FFFFFF">In toy normal-form games this works cleanly because
best-responses can be solved exactly.</font>

14
00:01:00,151 --> 00:01:04,114
<font color="#FFFFFF">But real-world games are temporal and partially observed,</font>

15
00:01:04,194 --> 00:01:08,156
<font color="#FFFFFF">so best-responses can only be approximated with
expensive deep reinforcement learning.</font>

16
00:01:08,236 --> 00:01:12,199
<font color="#FFFFFF">This creates two failures: under a finite budget
you cannot tell a truly converged best-response</font>

17
00:01:12,279 --> 00:01:16,242
<font color="#FFFFFF">from one stuck at a local plateau,</font>

18
00:01:16,322 --> 00:01:20,285
<font color="#FFFFFF">so iterations get truncated prematurely and pollute
the population with weak policies;</font>

19
00:01:20,365 --> 00:01:24,327
<font color="#FFFFFF">and every new policy relearns basic skills from scratch,</font>

20
00:01:24,407 --> 00:01:28,370
<font color="#FFFFFF">which becomes intractable as opponents grow stronger."</font>

21
00:01:28,750 --> 00:01:33,528
<font color="#FFFFFF">"The need for a population of strategies is rooted
in game theory: in a purely cyclic</font>

22
00:01:33,608 --> 00:01:37,128
<font color="#FFFFFF">game like rock-paper-scissors a single strategy is
meaningless,</font>

23
00:01:37,208 --> 00:01:40,615
<font color="#FFFFFF">since improving against one opponent means losing
to another.</font>

24
00:01:40,695 --> 00:01:47,073
<font color="#FFFFFF">Prior frameworks handle this by training policies
one at a time and discarding the shared knowledge between them.</font>

25
00:01:47,153 --> 00:01:51,645
<font color="#FFFFFF">NeuPL's insight is that these policies overlap
enormously they share perception,</font>

26
00:01:51,725 --> 00:01:55,531
<font color="#FFFFFF">memory, and motor skills so training them
independently is wasteful.</font>

27
00:01:55,611 --> 00:02:01,589
<font color="#FFFFFF">If a single model could hold the whole population
and condition its behaviour on which opponents it faces,</font>

28
00:02:01,669 --> 00:02:06,447
<font color="#FFFFFF">early skills learned against weak opponents would
directly bootstrap the discovery of</font>

29
00:02:06,527 --> 00:02:08,390
<font color="#FFFFFF">exploiters to much stronger ones."</font>

30
00:02:08,770 --> 00:02:12,142
<font color="#FFFFFF">"NeuPL makes three contributions.</font>

31
00:02:12,222 --> 00:02:15,593
<font color="#FFFFFF">First,</font>

32
00:02:15,673 --> 00:02:19,045
<font color="#FFFFFF">it is a single conditional model that represents a
whole population of policies,</font>

33
00:02:19,125 --> 00:02:22,497
<font color="#FFFFFF">conditioned on a meta-game mixture strategy that
specifies which opponents each policy should beat.</font>

34
00:02:22,577 --> 00:02:25,948
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second,</font>

35
00:02:26,028 --> 00:02:29,400
<font color="#FFFFFF">it is a unifying framework: by choosing the
interaction graph you recover self-play,</font>

36
00:02:29,480 --> 00:02:32,852
<font color="#FFFFFF">fictitious play, or PSRO-Nash as special cases,</font>

37
00:02:32,932 --> 00:02:36,303
<font color="#FFFFFF">and you can even express cyclic graphs that PSRO cannot.</font>

38
00:02:36,383 --> 00:02:39,755
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third, it comes with convergence guarantees for grounded,</font>

39
00:02:39,835 --> 00:02:43,207
<font color="#FFFFFF">lower-triangular interaction graphs and a suitable
meta-graph solver,</font>

40
00:02:43,287 --> 00:02:46,658
<font color="#FFFFFF">NeuPL provably converges to an N-step best-response
and, with large enough N,</font>

41
00:02:46,738 --> 00:02:50,110
<font color="#FFFFFF">to a normal-form Nash equilibrium."</font>

42
00:02:50,490 --> 00:02:54,059
<font color="#FFFFFF">"Concretely, NeuPL trains one opponent-conditioned
network.</font>

43
00:02:54,139 --> 00:02:58,760
<font color="#FFFFFF">Each policy index i is paired with a meta-strategy
vector sigma-i that says,</font>

44
00:02:58,840 --> 00:03:03,398
<font color="#FFFFFF">as a probability distribution, which other policies
it should play against.</font>

45
00:03:03,478 --> 00:03:07,481
<font color="#FFFFFF">The network maximizes discounted return under a
double expectation</font>

46
00:03:07,561 --> 00:03:09,460
<font color="#FFFFFF">first over the sampled opponent,</font>

47
00:03:09,540 --> 00:03:12,738
<font color="#FFFFFF">then over the game dynamics induced by both policies.</font>

48
00:03:12,818 --> 00:03:15,397
<font color="#FFFFFF">To optimize this by reinforcement learning,</font>

49
00:03:15,477 --> 00:03:19,541
<font color="#FFFFFF">NeuPL jointly trains an opponent-conditioned
action-value function,</font>

50
00:03:19,621 --> 00:03:25,170
<font color="#FFFFFF">and additionally learns a payoff estimator that
predicts the expected outcome of any policy</font>

51
00:03:25,250 --> 00:03:27,705
<font color="#FFFFFF">pairing directly from the value function,</font>

52
00:03:27,785 --> 00:03:32,839
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the full payoff matrix can be evaluated cheaply
without replaying every matchup.</font>

53
00:03:32,919 --> 00:03:36,921
<font color="#FFFFFF">The interaction graph that assigns opponents can be
a fixed matrix</font>

54
00:03:37,001 --> 00:03:39,395
<font color="#FFFFFF">reproducing fictitious play or adaptive,</font>

55
00:03:39,475 --> 00:03:44,467
<font color="#FFFFFF">recomputed from the learned payoffs by a meta-graph
solver, reproducing PSRO-Nash.</font>

56
00:03:44,547 --> 00:03:49,662
<font color="#FFFFFF">Because the graph, not a handcrafted schedule,
controls which objectives are active,</font>

57
00:03:49,742 --> 00:03:54,610
<font color="#FFFFFF">the number of genuinely distinct policies grows on
its own as the game demands."</font>

58
00:03:54,990 --> 00:03:58,706
<font color="#FFFFFF">"NeuPL is validated across three domains chosen to
span the difficulty spectrum.</font>

59
00:03:58,786 --> 00:04:02,503
<font color="#FFFFFF">Rock-paper-scissors is the classic purely cyclic
normal-form game,</font>

60
00:04:02,583 --> 00:04:06,299
<font color="#FFFFFF">where the learned population can be visualized
directly on the strategy simplex.</font>

61
00:04:06,379 --> 00:04:10,095
<font color="#FFFFFF">Running-with-scissors lifts this into a spatiotemporal,</font>

62
00:04:10,175 --> 00:04:13,892
<font color="#FFFFFF">partially observed setting: players move on a grid,
collect rock, paper,</font>

63
00:04:13,972 --> 00:04:17,688
<font color="#FFFFFF">and scissors resources,</font>

64
00:04:17,768 --> 00:04:21,485
<font color="#FFFFFF">and must infer a hidden opponent inventory from a
narrow first-person view.</font>

65
00:04:21,565 --> 00:04:25,281
<font color="#FFFFFF">Finally,</font>

66
00:04:25,361 --> 00:04:29,077
<font color="#FFFFFF">MuJoCo Football is a large-scale Game-of-Skills
where two-versus-two teams must simultaneously</font>

67
00:04:29,157 --> 00:04:32,874
<font color="#FFFFFF">master continuous motor control and coordinated team play,</font>

68
00:04:32,954 --> 00:04:36,670
<font color="#FFFFFF">a regime where handcrafted PSRO truncation is
especially fragile."</font>

69
00:04:37,050 --> 00:04:41,254
<font color="#FFFFFF">"The headline result is that NeuPL is both more
efficient and more robust than comparable PSRO baselines.</font>

70
00:04:41,334 --> 00:04:45,538
<font color="#FFFFFF">With a maximum population of eight policies,</font>

71
00:04:45,618 --> 00:04:49,822
<font color="#FFFFFF">a NeuPL population successfully exploits PSRO
populations of the same eight policies</font>

72
00:04:49,902 --> 00:04:54,106
<font color="#FFFFFF">even when each PSRO iteration was given twice as
many gradient steps.</font>

73
00:04:54,186 --> 00:04:58,390
<font color="#FFFFFF">Crucially,</font>

74
00:04:58,470 --> 00:05:02,674
<font color="#FFFFFF">the gain in relative population performance
coincides with growth in the effective population size,</font>

75
00:05:02,754 --> 00:05:06,958
<font color="#FFFFFF">from five up to eight distinct policies,</font>

76
00:05:07,038 --> 00:05:11,242
<font color="#FFFFFF">showing that the improvement comes from genuinely
discovering new strategies rather than overfitting.</font>

77
00:05:11,322 --> 00:05:15,526
<font color="#FFFFFF">Both continued-training and from-scratch PSRO
variants prove equally exploitable,</font>

78
00:05:15,606 --> 00:05:19,810
<font color="#FFFFFF">suggesting they fail to build reusable representations."</font>

79
00:05:20,190 --> 00:05:22,956
<font color="#FFFFFF">"A key ablation isolates the role of transfer.</font>

80
00:05:23,036 --> 00:05:28,277
<font color="#FFFFFF">NeuPL agents are re-initialized either from scratch
or by transferring the encoder and</font>

81
00:05:28,357 --> 00:05:32,052
<font color="#FFFFFF">memory components of a network trained to epoch one
thousand,</font>

82
00:05:32,132 --> 00:05:36,259
<font color="#FFFFFF">then tasked with beating fixed Nash mixtures of
increasing strength.</font>

83
00:05:36,339 --> 00:05:39,291
<font color="#FFFFFF">Against an easily exploitable two-policy mixture,</font>

84
00:05:39,371 --> 00:05:43,807
<font color="#FFFFFF">even the from-scratch agent eventually finds a
counter, just more slowly.</font>

85
00:05:43,887 --> 00:05:47,458
<font color="#FFFFFF">But against competent mixtures over four or seven
policies,</font>

86
00:05:47,538 --> 00:05:51,975
<font color="#FFFFFF">the randomly initialized agent fails outright
despite prolonged training,</font>

87
00:05:52,055 --> 00:05:55,563
<font color="#FFFFFF">while the transferred agent readily discovers the exploit.</font>

88
00:05:55,643 --> 00:05:59,647
<font color="#FFFFFF">This is NeuPL's most striking property: as the
population expands,</font>

89
00:05:59,727 --> 00:06:03,050
<font color="#FFFFFF">discovering new strategies becomes easier, not harder."</font>

90
00:06:03,430 --> 00:06:09,084
<font color="#FFFFFF">"To put numbers on it: a NeuPL population capped at
eight policies beats PSRO populations</font>

91
00:06:09,164 --> 00:06:13,529
<font color="#FFFFFF">of eight policies even when PSRO trained twice as
long per iteration.</font>

92
00:06:13,609 --> 00:06:16,234
<font color="#FFFFFF">As relative population performance climbs,</font>

93
00:06:16,314 --> 00:06:20,551
<font color="#FFFFFF">the effective number of distinct policies grows
from five to eight,</font>

94
00:06:20,631 --> 00:06:25,511
<font color="#FFFFFF">and across different maximum caps the effective
size saturates around twelve.</font>

95
00:06:25,591 --> 00:06:30,279
<font color="#FFFFFF">The transfer study spans Nash mixtures over two,
four, and seven policies,</font>

96
00:06:30,359 --> 00:06:31,889
<font color="#FFFFFF">each repeated five times.</font>

97
00:06:31,969 --> 00:06:39,040
<font color="#FFFFFF">Together these establish NeuPL as more
sample-efficient and more robust than the standard iterative baselines."</font>

98
00:06:39,420 --> 00:06:44,500
<font color="#FFFFFF">"The one-line takeaway: represent the whole
population in a single conditional model,</font>

99
00:06:44,580 --> 00:06:47,961
<font color="#FFFFFF">let the interaction graph decide who trains against whom,</font>

100
00:06:48,041 --> 00:06:50,693
<font color="#FFFFFF">and skills transfer for free across policies.</font>

101
00:06:50,773 --> 00:06:55,307
<font color="#FFFFFF">This makes population learning cheaper, gives it
convergence guarantees, and</font>

102
00:06:55,387 --> 00:06:59,374
<font color="#FFFFFF">most surprisingly makes novel strategies more
accessible, not less,</font>

103
00:06:59,454 --> 00:07:01,377
<font color="#FFFFFF">as the neural population expands,</font>

104
00:07:01,457 --> 00:07:05,020
<font color="#FFFFFF">from rock-paper-scissors all the way up to MuJoCo
Football."</font>
